 "step_sd_note": "per-step load differs (tokens 27052/31454/35506) -> only paired diffs are comparable",
 "findings": [
  "CLEAN ISOLATION (one process per mode) removes the registration-stacking artefact: hit attribution is now stable (each routed mode independently hits 1584 = 528/step x 3 steps over 12/12 shapes) and no longer swaps.",
  "py_route vs native over 6 real steps: [-18.8, -3.3, -7.9, -3.2, 0.1, -11.1] ms -> median -5.6 ms, 5/6 NEGATIVE (faster). This is the first direction-consistent result; all earlier runs (registration stacking) flipped sign.",
  "native_contig vs native: [35.4, 10.3, 30.7, 41.2, 22.7, 34.0] ms -> median 32.4 ms, 0/6 negative. Explicit contiguous() copy is consistently MORE expensive than the transposed-view penalty it removes -> this native-side alternative is CLOSED.",
  "KERNEL IDENTITY CONFIRMED: py_route runs Triton mm_kernel_general 528x (the whitelisted dW) + native gemm_ktype0 tile128x128 2688x; native_contig and native both run that native gemm 3216x (=2688+528). All other kernel counts are identical across the three modes -> both routed paths perform the SAME computation on the SAME call set.",
  "HOST DISPATCH OVERHEAD IS REAL: host aten::mm events 5,776 (native) -> 11,024 (py_route) -> 11,552 (native_contig). The Python override roughly doubles host mm events. But the whole-step delta is still slightly negative for py_route, i.e. the dW kernel gain (~15-33 ms/step on the measured shapes) slightly EXCEEDS the host cost. The earlier '+303 ms/step host time' figure was profiler-on diagnostic CPU time, not wall time, and was inflated by registration stacking.",
  "NATIVE TRANSPOSED-VIEW ALTERNATIVES (same product): gy.t()@x 0.485 ms; (x.t()@gy).t() 0.519; contiguous() copy inside 0.637; matmul 0.520 -> no native path beats A. No dedicated transposed-view fast path exists on this stack.",
  "LIBRARY HANDLE BUG FOUND AND FIXED: torch.library.Library(...) must be kept alive; when the returned object is garbage-collected the registration is silently dropped (first run of route_isolated.py showed hits=0 and identical aten::mm counts in all modes)."
 ],
 "verdict": {
  "question": "can the dW kernel's local gain cross the call boundary and reach the whole step?",
  "answer": "YES, marginally: with a clean single registration the dW Python route is faster than native on 5 of 6 real steps (median -5.6 ms, range -18.8..+0.1), i.e. roughly 0.26% of a ~2.13 s step. The host dispatch cost of the Python override is real but is slightly smaller than the kernel gain.",
  "caveats": [
   "6 paired steps only; one step is +0.1 (neutral); the margin is within the step-to-step spread, so this is 'small, direction-mostly-consistent gain', NOT a guaranteed speedup",
   "host-time figures are profiler-on diagnostic (correlation evidence), not wall-clock decomposition",
   "no library-level/C++ hook is available in this environment (FlagGems mm is a Python function) - the remaining host cost cannot be removed here"
  ],
  "training_impact": "dW Python route: precision bitwise-identical (hit-step recompute PASS), small consistent whole-step gain (~5.6 ms median, ~0.26%). Not adopted as deployable (hardware_status unverified; gain within noise); recorded as the one candidate with a positive, reproducible-sign result. Native contiguous() alternative closed."
 }
